Analysis:
- src/dotenv/cli.py:213 — `Popen(command, universal_newlines=True, bufsize=0, shell=False, env=cmd_env)`. shell=False with an argv list; no string interpolation into a shell. FALSE POSITIVE.
- src/dotenv/cli.py:218 — `os.execvpe(command[0], args=command, env=cmd_env)`. No shell involved; argv list passed directly to execve. FALSE POSITIVE.
- Also verified the full `run` command implementation (lines 160-182): `commandline` comes from `@click.argument("commandline", nargs=-1, type=click.UNPROCESSED)`, which is by design — the `dotenv run -- <cmd>` subcommand is intentionally documented as "Run command with environment variables present", i.e. a thin wrapper equivalent to `env`. The operator invoking the CLI supplies their own command; there is no privilege boundary crossed, no shell reinterpretation, and Click does not split/expand metacharacters (UNPROCESSED + nargs=-1 yields raw argv tokens).
- Lines 181, 187 (docstring), and 210 ("win32") are string literals/prompt messages adjacent to the sink, not injectable flows. FALSE POSITIVES.
- `.env` values are only merged into the child process environment dict (`cmd_env.update(env)`), they are never concatenated into the command string, so a malicious .env file cannot escalate to arbitrary command execution beyond what the invoked program itself does with its env.

Conclusion: no CWE-78 (nor CWE-77/88/74) exploitable path exists; the flagged code is correct, intentional, shell-free command execution. 5 findings submitted, all classified false positive.
